/*
 * The Examples page, and the library sketches in
 * platform/libraries/<library>/examples/ registered for it, with the formatted
 * description shown above each sketch's source. Doxygen does not read comments
 * in the sketches themselves, so keep each block here in step with that
 * sketch's header comment, with the list on the Examples page, with the
 * sketches under Examples in DoxygenLayout.xml (the side menu) and with the
 * Included libraries table in docs/index.html.
 *
 * The Examples page replaces Doxygen's own list (hidden in DoxygenLayout.xml),
 * which can only show file names such as AnalogMillivolts.ino. For the same
 * reason each sketch's page title is set by the hidden example-title span at
 * the start of its block, which a script in footer.html moves into the title.
 */

/**
@page example_sketches Examples

Example sketches for Phyduino boards. Each page below explains what the sketch
does, how to wire it and what it prints, followed by its source. The same
sketches are in the Arduino IDE under **File → Examples → Examples for
Phyduino Pico** once Phyduino Pico is selected: **Phyduino** for the analog, USB-PD, multicore,
FreeRTOS and CMSIS-RTOS2 sketches, **SPI** and **Wire** for those libraries.

| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref AnalogMillivolts.ino "Analog Millivolts" | Every analog pin in millivolts |
| @ref AnalogAveraging.ino "Analog Averaging" | How averaging steadies a reading |
| @ref AnalogDifferential.ino "Analog Differential" | The voltage between two pins |
| @ref AnalogReadMultiple.ino "Analog Read Multiple" | All six analog pins at once, in the Serial Plotter |
| @ref AnalogBurst.ino "Analog Burst" | A fixed-rate burst of samples, to see a waveform |
| @ref AnalogBurstBackground.ino "Analog Burst Background" | Continuous sampling in the background |
| @ref UsbPdPower.ino "USB-PD Power" | Asking a USB-PD charger for a higher voltage |
| @ref MulticoreBasics.ino "Multicore Basics" | Two sketches at once, one on each core |
| @ref MulticoreFifo.ino "Multicore FIFO" | Handing work to the second core through rp2040.fifo |
| @ref FreeRtosTasks.ino "FreeRTOS Tasks" | FreeRTOS tasks side by side, and the task list (needs FreeRTOS) |
| @ref FreeRtosQueue.ino "FreeRTOS Queue" | Passing measurements between tasks through a queue (needs FreeRTOS) |
| @ref CmsisRtos2Threads.ino "CMSIS-RTOS2 Threads" | Threads and a message queue with ARM's CMSIS-RTOS2 API (needs FreeRTOS + CMSIS-RTOS2) |
| @ref SpiLoopback.ino "SPI Loopback" | An SPI loopback test |
| @ref I2CScanner.ino "I2C Scanner" | Finding the devices on the I2C bus |
| @ref PeripheralReceiver.ino "Peripheral Receiver" | The board as an I2C peripheral |
*/

/**
 * @example AnalogMillivolts.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Millivolts example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the voltage on every analog pin, in millivolts, once a second, and
 * the full scale of each pin at start-up.
 *
 * A0-A2 (RP2040 ADC) read 0-3300 mV and A3-A5 (on-board MAX11635) read
 * 0-2048 mV; analogReadMillivolts() and analogFullScaleMillivolts() handle
 * the difference, so the same code works on every pin.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A0 1650 mV   A1 3 mV   A2 0 mV   A3 1024 mV   A4 0 mV   A5 0 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example AnalogAveraging.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Averaging example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Shows how averaging steadies a reading: for averaging 1, 4, 8, 16 and 32,
 * takes 100 readings of A0 and A3 and prints the lowest, the highest and the
 * spread, in 12-bit ADC steps.
 *
 * analogReadAveraging() applies to every analog pin: A3-A5 average inside
 * the external ADC, A0-A2 take the samples one after another.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Optional: a steady voltage, e.g. a potentiometer wiper, on A0 (0-3.3 V)
 * and A3 (0-2.048 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Averaging 1:   A0 2041-2058 (spread 17)   A3 1022-1025 (spread 3)
 * Averaging 32:   A0 2048-2050 (spread 2)   A3 1023-1023 (spread 0)
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example AnalogDifferential.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Differential example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Measures the voltage between two pins twice a second, with
 * analogReadDifferentialMillivolts():
 * - A3 - A4: true differential; the on-board ADC samples both pins at the
 *   same instant, so noise common to both cancels. ±1024 mV in 0.5 mV steps.
 * - A0 - A1: computed from two readings a few µs apart; fine for slow signals.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * The two points to measure between, e.g. the ends of a resistor, on A3 and
 * A4 (each 0-2.048 V) or A0 and A1 (each 0-3.3 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A3 - A4: 251 mV    A0 - A1: -12 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example AnalogReadMultiple.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Read Multiple example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads all six analog pins together with analogReadMultiple() and streams
 * them, in millivolts, to the Serial Plotter 20 times a second. A3-A5 are
 * read in one scan of the external ADC, A0-A2 back to back on the RP2040 ADC.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Tools → Serial Plotter at 115200 baud, one line per reading:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A0:1650,A1:3,A2:0,A3:1024,A4:0,A5:0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example AnalogBurst.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Burst example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Captures 500 samples of A0 at 20000 samples per second with
 * analogReadBurst(), plots them in the Serial Plotter and prints the rate,
 * lowest, highest and mean. The hardware takes the samples at evenly spaced
 * times, so the plot shows the true waveform.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A wire from D2 to A0: the sketch plays a 500 Hz test tone on D2. Or a
 * signal generator on A0 (0-3.3 V), and remove the tone() line.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Tools → Serial Plotter at 115200 baud: a square wave, then a line such as
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Rate 20000 Hz, lowest 3, highest 4093, mean 2049
 * @endcode
 *
 * @par Notes
 * Highest rates: A0-A2 500000 samples/s, A3-A5 300000 samples/s. Change
 * `PIN`, `RATE_HZ` and `SAMPLES` at the top of the sketch.
 */

/**
 * @example AnalogBurstBackground.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Burst Background example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Samples A3 continuously in the background with analogStartBurst() and
 * analogBurstDone(), in blocks of 1000 samples at 10000 samples per second.
 * One buffer fills while the other is processed ("double buffering"), and
 * the built-in LED blinks to show that loop() is never blocked. Prints the
 * mean and the RMS of each block, in millivolts.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, ten lines a second, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Mean 1023.6 mV, RMS 0.41 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example UsbPdPower.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>USB-PD Power example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the board's USB-PD power profiles, the charger's contract and the
 * measured VBUS once a second. Send 5, 9, 12, 15 or 20 over Serial to ask a
 * USB-PD charger for up to that many volts, with stusb4500::boardProfiles()
 * and stusb4500::setProfiles().
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A USB-PD charger on the USB-C port for anything above 5 V; a PC port gives
 * 5 V and no contract (offer 0). With a charger on the port, use Serial1
 * (D1 TX, D0 RX, 115200) through a USB-serial adapter or a second board
 * running a serial bridge: the sketch writes to, and reads from, both.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Asking for up to 12 V
 * profiles: 5.0V/2.00A 9.0V/1.00A 12.0V/0.83A   contract: offer 3, 830 mA   VBUS: 12.04 V
 * @endcode
 *
 * @warning VBUS also feeds the VIN pin, so VIN follows the contract.
 *          Disconnect anything on VIN that can't take the voltage asked for.
 *          The request lasts until power-down; then Tools > USB-PD Power
 *          applies again.
 */

/**
 * @example MulticoreBasics.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Multicore Basics example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Runs two sketches at once, one on each core of the RP2040. setup() and
 * loop() run on core 0 as usual; defining setup1() and loop1() starts core 1,
 * which runs them at the same time. Core 0 prints a count every second while
 * core 1 blinks the LED: neither one's delay() holds up the other.
 *
 * Works with Tools > Operating System set to None or FreeRTOS (SMP).
 * See @ref core_multicore.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud; the LED flashes twice a second:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * setup() runs on core 0
 * loop() on core 0: 0
 * loop() on core 0: 1
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example MulticoreFifo.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Multicore FIFO example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Hands slow work to the second core and gets the answers back through
 * `rp2040.fifo`. Core 0 sends four numbers; core 1 counts the primes below
 * each one and sends back the count and how long it took. Core 0 is free in
 * the meantime: it keeps the LED blinking and prints each answer as it
 * arrives.
 *
 * A value pushed on one core is popped on the other; pop() waits until there
 * is one, pop_nb() and available() do not wait. See @ref core_multicore.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud. The counts are always these; the times,
 * shown here as `<t>`, depend on Tools > CPU Speed, and the last one is
 * several seconds:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Work sent to core 1.
 * Primes below 1000: 168 (<t> ms on core 1)
 * Primes below 10000: 1229 (<t> ms on core 1)
 * Primes below 100000: 9592 (<t> ms on core 1)
 * Primes below 1000000: 78498 (<t> ms on core 1)
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example FreeRtosTasks.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>FreeRTOS Tasks example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Runs FreeRTOS tasks side by side. Needs Tools > Operating System >
 * FreeRTOS (SMP).
 *
 * setup() starts two tasks: one blinks the LED, the other prints a count.
 * loop() is a task too; every five seconds it prints the list of tasks with
 * vTaskListTasks(). Each task waits with vTaskDelay() (delay() does the same
 * with FreeRTOS), which leaves the cores free for the others. See
 * @ref core_freertos.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud: a "countTask on core 0: n" or "countTask on
 * core 1: n" line every second (the count task may run on either core), and
 * every five seconds one line per task: loop, blink, count, the two idle
 * tasks IDLE0 and IDLE1, and the FreeRTOS timer task Tmr Svc. The columns:
 *
 * | Column | Meaning |
 * |---|---|
 * | Name | Task name |
 * | State | X running, R ready, B blocked (waiting), S suspended, D deleted |
 * | Prio | Priority, 0 (idle) to 55 |
 * | Stack | The least free stack the task has had, in 4-byte words: near 0 means too small |
 * | Num | Task number, in order of creation |
 * | Cores | Cores the task may run on: 0x1 core 0, 0x2 core 1, 0xffffffff either |
 */

/**
 * @example FreeRtosQueue.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>FreeRTOS Queue example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Passes measurements from one FreeRTOS task to another through a queue.
 * Needs Tools > Operating System > FreeRTOS (SMP).
 *
 * A sampling task reads A0 exactly every 100 ms (vTaskDelayUntil()) and puts
 * each reading on a queue; loop() waits for readings and prints them. The
 * sampling task has the higher priority, so its timing holds even while
 * loop() is busy printing. See @ref core_freertos.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A voltage to measure on A0, e.g. a potentiometer wiper (0-3.3 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * 1203 ms: A0 = 1650 mV
 * 1303 ms: A0 = 1651 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example CmsisRtos2Threads.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>CMSIS-RTOS2 Threads example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Runs threads with ARM's CMSIS-RTOS2 API, which works the same on any
 * CMSIS-RTOS2 system. Needs Tools > Operating System > FreeRTOS (SMP) +
 * CMSIS-RTOS2.
 *
 * The kernel is already running when setup() starts, so osKernelInitialize()
 * and osKernelStart() are not needed. setup() prints the kernel's name
 * (osKernelGetInfo()) and starts two threads with osThreadNew(): one blinks
 * the LED with osDelay(), the other reads A0 exactly every 100 ms
 * (osDelayUntil()) and puts each reading in a message queue. The sampling
 * thread runs at osPriorityAboveNormal, above loop()'s osPriorityNormal, so
 * its timing holds while loop() waits for readings with osMessageQueueGet()
 * and prints them. See @ref core_cmsis_rtos2.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A voltage to measure on A0, e.g. a potentiometer wiper (0-3.3 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud; the LED flashes once a second:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Kernel: FreeRTOS V11.1.0+
 * 1203 ms: A0 = 1650 mV
 * 1303 ms: A0 = 1651 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example SpiLoopback.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>SPI Loopback example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * SPI loopback test: sends a counter with SPI.transfer() every 500 ms and
 * prints the byte that comes back. With MOSI wired to MISO, every byte sent
 * should come straight back.
 *
 * Uses PhyduinoSPI::beginTransaction() with 1 MHz, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * D11 (MOSI) to D12 (MISO).
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sent 7 received 7
 * @endcode
 * With D11 and D12 not connected, the received value is usually 0 or 255.
 */

/**
 * @example I2CScanner.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>I2C Scanner example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Scans the I2C bus on the SDA/SCL header pins every 5 s and prints every
 * address from 0x08 to 0x77 that answers.
 *
 * Each address is probed with PhyduinoWire::beginTransmission() and
 * PhyduinoWire::endTransmission() with no data; 0 means a device answered.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * One or more I2C devices on SDA, SCL, 3.3 V and GND.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Device at 0x3C
 * 1 device(s) found.
 * @endcode
 *
 * @note The board has no I2C pull-up resistors, only the RP2040's weak
 *       internal ones. Add 4.7 kOhm resistors to 3.3 V for long wires or
 *       400 kHz.
 */

/**
 * @example PeripheralReceiver.ino
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Peripheral Receiver example sketch</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Makes the board an I2C peripheral at address 0x42. Prints the bytes a
 * controller writes, and answers each read with a counter that goes up by one.
 *
 * Uses PhyduinoWire::begin(uint8_t) with PhyduinoWire::onReceive() and
 * PhyduinoWire::onRequest().
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * SDA, SCL and GND to another board running a controller sketch.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud: the bytes the controller wrote, as text.
 *
 * @note The callbacks run inside the I2C interrupt, so keep them short.
 */
